# macOS explicit session takeover

Status: implementation and automated qualification; not merged or installed.

An active first client continues streaming while the Mac is locked. A second
client previously reached display preparation before launch and received a
generic resolution HTTP 409. This is active ownership, not a stale lease or an
unavailable display mode.

Keep display preparation before Client decoder initialization. Return a typed
active-session conflict containing a random per-stream UUID. Only an explicit
fresh GUI sign-in may ask Take Over/Cancel. Wait on the authentication worker,
never on the GUI; Cancel, shutdown and the two-minute consent deadline decline.
CLI and automatic reconnect do not implicitly replace a stream.

After consent, send that exact stream UUID with the existing authenticated
display request. Require the current scope, peer-bound setup token and matching
verified account UID/UUID. A delayed response must not replace a newer stream.
The normal stop path releases held input and drains capture/encoder/QUIC before
the replacement display is prepared. The old client receives the existing
terminal takeover notice. A fifteen-second setup reservation prevents a racing
reconnect from stealing the next launch or superseding its token behind NAT.
Do not reset OS permissions, unlock the Mac, reboot or create another service.

Automated gates:

- Shared exact feature mask and topology fixture on Host and Client.
- Authentication: same-account acceptance; wrong peer/account/lease denial;
  reservation conflict, expiry, revocation and same-peer token preservation.
- Real TLS/QUIC with synthetic display: conflict, no-consent unchanged state,
  stale UUID denial, terminal old receiver, replacement geometry and launch.
- Qt: strict conflict parsing, accept/cancel/Escape, late clicks/timeouts and
  responsive UI while the worker awaits the user's choice.
- Existing platform suites and combined transport/build-policy gates.

Live acceptance remains separate: use two clients with different resolutions
and Retina scales, with the Host both locked and unlocked. Cancel must leave the
first stream untouched; takeover must preserve the logged-in account, stop old
input and present the new size correctly. The old client must not reconnect over
the replacement. Also test normal reconnect, login/logout and a rejected
different-account takeover. Linux login/input behavior must remain unchanged.
